# core/label-collisions - Which elements share a name across models?
#
# Answers: elements whose normalised label matches another element in a DIFFERENT
# model, grouped by that normalised label, with each side's own type and model.
#
# Does NOT prove ANYTHING about identity. These are CANDIDATES. Two applications
# called "Billing" may be one system modelled twice, two different systems, or a
# system and a report about it. Cross-tool identity in this package is authored and
# human-reviewed, never derived - core/identity-audit reads the assertions, and this
# template deliberately does not produce any.
#
# It exists because the alternative was worse. When no identity role is bound,
# core/identity-audit is correctly refused, which leaves nothing between "asserted
# identity" and eyeballing labels - and a field session did reconcile five resources
# named some form of "Errata Hub" across four notations by pure label reasoning, in
# an answer. Making the candidate set visible and explicitly un-asserted is safer
# than leaving the same reasoning to happen off the record.
#
# The catalogue attaches a caveat to every result from this template, so the warning
# travels with the rows rather than staying in the documentation.
#
# Normalisation folds case and every run of non-alphanumeric characters to a single
# space, then trims: "Errata Hub", "errata-hub" and "ERRATA_HUB" collide, while
# "Errata Hub 2" does not. That is deliberately crude. A cleverer normaliser would
# make the candidate set larger and its rules harder to explain, and the reader is
# the one deciding.
#
# ?models is the number of distinct models the normalised label appears in, repeated
# on each row, so a two-way collision is visibly different from a five-way one.
#
# Only cross-MODEL collisions are reported. Two elements with the same label inside
# one model are usually deliberate - a process step drawn in two places, or genuinely
# two things - and are a modelling question rather than an identity one.
#
# Membership comes from {{MEMBERSHIP:element}}, so "different model" means whatever
# the profile says membership means.
#
# Parameters: LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?normalised ?models ?element ?label ?type ?model
WHERE {
  {
    SELECT ?normalised (COUNT(DISTINCT ?model) AS ?models)
    WHERE {
      {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
        ?candidate a {{ROLE:element_class}} ;
                   {{PATH:label}} ?candidateLabel .
        {{MEMBERSHIP:candidate}}
      {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
      BIND(REPLACE(REPLACE(LCASE(STR(?candidateLabel)), "[^a-z0-9]+", " "), "^ +| +$", "") AS ?normalised)
    }
    GROUP BY ?normalised
    HAVING(COUNT(DISTINCT ?model) > 1)
  }
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic2}}
    ?element a {{ROLE:element_class}} ;
             {{PATH:label}} ?label .
    {{MEMBERSHIP:element}}
    OPTIONAL {
      ?element a ?type .
      FILTER(?type != {{ROLE:concept_class}} && ?type != {{ROLE:element_class}})
    }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  FILTER(REPLACE(REPLACE(LCASE(STR(?label)), "[^a-z0-9]+", " "), "^ +| +$", "") = ?normalised)
}
ORDER BY DESC(?models) ?normalised ?model ?element
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
